# jemdoc: notime
= Research

I work toward a *predictive science of deep learning*: explaining learning mechanisms, predicting training dynamics, and guiding algorithm design. My research moves between mathematical analysis of tractable models and large-scale experiments, with each informing and challenging the other.

# For broader reflections on deep learning theory, see my [./research-perspective.html research perspective].


== LLM Pretraining: Modeling, Prediction, and Control

We seek a macroscopic description of LLM pretraining through effective equations that capture the mechanisms governing loss dynamics. This approach helps explain training behavior and improve training efficiency.

- +Functional scaling laws (FSL).+ We introduced FSL: effective equations that describe learning dynamics through the competition between signal learning and noise forgetting  ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19189 NeurIPS 2025]).  By analyzing how training choices alter this competition, we can explain and predict their effects:
  -- Momentum ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02728 arXiv 2026])
  -- SignSGD versus SGD ([https://neurips.cc/virtual/2026/loc/sydney/poster/150706 NeurIPS 2026])
  -- Batch-size schedules ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14208 ICLR 2026])
  -- Learning-rate schedules ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06797 COLT 2026]).

- +ELR collapse.+ We identify the effective learning rate---the learning rate divided by parameter norm---as a more fundamental variable than the nominal learning rate for describing LLM loss dynamics ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24814 arXiv 2026]).

- +Optimizer design.+ We develop optimizers for efficient LLM pretraining ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19002 ICML 2025], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24275 ICML 2026]).

For more on FSL, see my *[doc/LLM-pretraining-theory.pdf talk slides]*.


== SGD Dynamics: Stability, Sharpness, and Generalization

Why does SGD always find minima that generalize well, even when the model is overparameterized? We introduced *dynamical stability* as a mechanism for SGD's implicit regularization. Our work links stability constraints and SGD's noise geometry to minima selection, sharpness, and generalization.

- Dynamical stability and minima selection ([https://papers.nips.cc/paper/8049-how-sgd-selects-the-global-minima-in-over-parameterized-learning-a-dynamical-stability-perspective NeurIPS 2018]).
- Noise alignment and flat minima ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02628 NeurIPS 2022]).
- Stability and generalization ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17490 ICML 2023]).
- SGD noise geometry ([https://openreview.net/forum?id=F6wmIilZXG TMLR 2026]).

For an overview of this line of work, see my [doc/dynamical-stability.pdf talk slides].

== Neural Network Theory: Approximation and Estimation

We introduced and named *Barron spaces* for two-layer neural networks, establishing approximation and generalization results within this function-space framework. Our broader work studies how neural networks efficiently learn *high-dimensional functions* and when and why they outperform linear methods.

- Barron and flow-induced spaces ([https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08039 Constructive Approximation 2021]).
- Approximation–estimation duality ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05642 AoS 2025]).
- Neural networks versus linear methods ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04964 JMLR 2022]).
- Inductive biases of convolutional networks ([https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.08404 NeurIPS 2023]).

For more on approximation–estimation duality, see my [doc/function-space.pdf talk slides].

